Choose settings for your self-hosted Onetime Secret installation. The generated
files update as you make changes. This tool covers common choices; other
settings use the application's defaults.

The settings below ref󠄁l󠄁ec󠄁t󠄁 Onetime Secret v0.26.19󠄁. Have your installation method and datastore connection details ready.
You won't enter passwords or API keys here: add those values on your server
after downloading the files. You can bookmark or share this page to save your choices.

{% config-generator /%}

## Apply configuration

These files are a starting point for the options above. They do not install
databases, configure an identity provider, or replace the setup steps for your [Docker](/en/install/docker/) or [Linux](/en/install/linux/) installation.

1. **Save the YAML settings.** For a new source installation,
   save the generated files as `etc/config.yaml` and `etc/auth.yaml` in the application directory. The application merges these settings with
   its shipped defaults. For an existing installation, back up your files and
   merge the generated keys into the matching sections; preserve your other
   settings and avoid duplicate YAML keys. Leave `etc/defaults/` unchanged.
2. **Complete your environment.** Add the generated entries to
   your existing server environment or `.env` file, filling in
   blank values and preserving existing secrets. Full authentication mode
   needs a SQL database connection and the independent `AUTH_SECRET`, `ARGON2_SECRET`, and `ACCOUNT_ID_SECRET` values.
   The [Docker setup guide](/en/install/docker/#what-must-be-set-before-either-stack-starts) explains secret generation and additional Compose requirements, including `VALKEY_PASSWORD`. Keep the completed file private and out of version control.
3. **Finish any provider or network setup.** For SSO, add the
   variables for the provider you use; the SSO switch alone does not configure
   a provider. For multiple regions, set `JURISDICTION` to this
   deployment's identifier and `JURISDICTIONS` to a list such as `EU:eu.example.com,CA:ca.example.com`. Set `VALKEY_URL` or `REDIS_URL` for your datastore; requiring TLS also needs a
   TLS-capable server and a `valkeys://` or `rediss://` URL.
   See the [environment variable reference](/en/reference/environment-variables/) for provider credentials and connection settings.
4. **Make the files available to the application.** A source
   installation reads YAML from its application directory; ensure its service
   also receives your environment values. In Docker, host files are not
   automatically available inside the container. Bind-mount your YAML files
   at `/app/etc/config.yaml` and `/app/etc/auth.yaml` for the application and any workers using them. Use read-only file mounts
   and retain existing data volumes. Follow your installation guide to pass
   environment values to each service; the generator's `.env` snippet is not a complete Compose environment.
5. **Reload and check the result.** Restart the application and
   workers after changing their configuration. Recreate Docker containers
   when changing their environment or mounts. Follow [Verify your install](/en/install/verify/), then create and
   retrieve a test secret and try any sign-in method you enabled.

For settings beyond this tool, use the [configuration files and precedence reference](/en/reference/configuration-files-and-precedence/).
